The Shrewd Dude wrote:
> Janis Papanagnou wrote:
>>Colin wrote:
>>
>>>I'm a novice player (almost always die before level 5 novice) and I
>>>have a lot of problems managing my inventory at the beginning of the
>>>game.  I tend to get burdened because I'm carrying around a lot of
>>>stuff that I haven't had the chance to identify yet.  Two questions: 1)
>>>what items should I leave behind initially, and 2) what's a safe way to
>>>leave things behind so that other creatures won't take them?
>>
>>Leave behind all the heavy items, mainly redundant armor and weapons, but
>>also spellbooks (if you are playing a spellcaster, you may want to read
>>them prior)
> 
> Spellbook-reading should be done with caution, as they can teleport
> you, blind you, paralyze you, and do other nasty things to you,
> especially if they're cursed. Any of those things can kill you easily
> in the early game.

Sure, but that's depending on what class you play - a wizard might be
one of the few who reads books in the first stage - and other factors,
like available shops to ID the level of the spellbook, altars or pets
to determine its b/u/c status, etc.

>>and plain gray stones.
> 
> You should be careful that you're not throwing away a luckstone.

Indeed. But the _plain_ stones will come in bigger heaps, while I
usually haven't seen luckstones to come in stacks larger than one or
two. (And even if there is some single gray stone I usually don't pick
it up to carry it in open inventory if I don't know its b/u/c status,
otherwise it only goes into a sack if I have one.)

>>You'll often find chests and boxes that are a safe place where monsters
>>won't look into. You may kick open chests that are locked (though that
>>can destroy glass items that have been in the container).
> 
> You can also use a lockpick, or try #forcing the lock.

True, keys and lockpicks are the apparent way to open these containers
if you found a key early on. Though #forcing might break your weapon;
some spare daggers might be helpful.

>>Another option
>>is pushing a boulder onto a stack of items.
> 
> Be careful with this, as giants CAN move boulders.

Weren't we talking about the early level 1-5 novice player game? He won't
see giants, I guess.

>>Locking items in a one square
>>niche is also possible.
> 
> Some creatures can open doors, and IIRC, doors can't be locked again
> once you've opened them.

No, that's wrong. If you have a key or lockpick (or an appropriate wand
or spell) then you can lock it again. You just cannot lock it with a
credit card.

> If you don't have the force bolt spell, a
> pickaxe, or some other way of breaking rock, you especially don't want
> to combine two methods of stuffing your stuff in a closet and blocking
